% A computed integer is looked up, tested for membership and tested for
% absence like any value.  One the value table does not hold -- 6 and 51
% below, which no fact states -- matches nothing and is absent from every
% relation, whether it is probed through an index on its column or tested
% for membership beside a column whose value the table does hold.  n/1 is
% the smallest relation, so each plan scans it first and computes Y from
% X, rather than testing a Y some other literal bound.
n(1). n(5). n(50).
m(2). m(100). m(a). m(b).
e(2, a). e(7, b). e(100, c). e(8, d).
pair(2, 1). pair(7, 8). pair(2, 3). pair(52, 7).

member(X) :- n(X), Y = X + 1, m(Y).
absent(X) :- n(X), Y = X + 1, not m(Y).
looked_up(X, Z) :- n(X), Y = X * 2, e(Y, Z).
keyed(X, W) :- n(X), Y = X + 1, pair(Y, W).
both(X) :- n(X), Y = X + 1, Z = X + 2, pair(Y, Z).
reversed(X) :- n(X), Y = X + 2, Z = X + 1, pair(Y, Z).

member(X)?
absent(X)?
looked_up(X, Z)?
keyed(X, W)?
both(X)?
reversed(X)?
